We use a temporary variable `temp` to swap the elements at indices `i` and `j`, and then call `swap(nums, start, i)` recursively to perform the next swap. We ensure that the swapped elements are swapped back to their original positions in the array by calling `swap(nums, start, i)` again before returning from the function.

The driver code calls the `permute` function with the array of integers and an empty result list, and returns the result list.

Complexity analysis:

The time complexity of this approach is O(N! \* N), where N is the length of the input array. The outer loop in the `permute` function iterates over all possible swaps of the first `start` elements of the array, and the recursive calls in the inner loop perform O(N) swaps each. The overall time complexity is the product of the number of swaps and the number of recursive calls.

The space complexity is O(N), since at any given time, we maintain a list of permutations, which can store up to N elements. Each permutation itself has a size of N, so the total space complexity is O(N^2).

